---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Caffeine: Dosing, Safety Limits & Sleep — Nawtch"
  description="Effective caffeine doses for performance and focus, FDA/EFSA daily safety limits, and how afternoon caffeine quietly disrupts sleep."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/caffeine/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Caffeine</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">The world's most-used performance aid, with real daily limits and a sleep cost if timed too late.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/">← Back to Supplements</a>
  </header>

  <p class="field-hint" style="margin:-4px 0 14px;">Not medical advice — pregnant people, those with anxiety or heart rhythm conditions, and anyone sensitive to stimulants should be more conservative than the ranges below.</p>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Caffeine blocks adenosine receptors in the brain — adenosine is a compound that builds up over the day and
        promotes drowsiness, so blocking it produces alertness and reduced perceived effort during exercise. It's
        also a mild stimulant of the central nervous system more broadly, which is behind its effects on focus,
        reaction time, and endurance performance.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">The Research</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Performance:</strong> Guest NS, et al. "International Society of Sports Nutrition Position Stand:
        Caffeine and Exercise Performance," <em>Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition</em>, 2021. Concludes caffeine reliably improves exercise performance, most consistently for aerobic endurance,
        at doses of 3–6mg/kg body mass, typically taken about 60 minutes before exercise; doses beyond roughly
        9mg/kg increase side effects without added benefit.
      </p>
      <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7777221/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the position stand →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Tolerance and timing:</strong> Drake C, Roehrs T, Shambroom J, Roth T. "Caffeine Effects on Sleep
        Taken 0, 3, or 6 Hours before Going to Bed," <em>Journal of Clinical Sleep Medicine</em>, 2013. In this
        trial, 400mg of caffeine taken even 6 hours before bedtime measurably reduced total sleep time by about an
        hour — notably, participants often didn't subjectively notice the disruption at that distance from
        bedtime, which is worth knowing if you drink coffee in the afternoon and don't feel like it's affecting
        your sleep.
      </p>
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24235903/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the study →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Tolerance to caffeine's stimulant effects builds with regular use, which is why habitual users often
        report needing more to feel the same alertness — the performance benefits documented above are generally
        still present even in habitual users, but the subjective "kick" fades faster than the underlying effect.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Typical Dosing & Safety Limits</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Effective performance dose:</strong> 3–6mg/kg body mass, per the ISSN position stand above.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Daily upper limit:</strong> the European Food Safety Authority's formal safety opinion puts
        habitual daily intake up to 400mg as not a safety concern for healthy adults, with single doses up to
        200mg also considered safe; pregnant and breastfeeding women are advised to limit intake to 200mg/day,
        and adolescents to about 3mg/kg/day. U.S. FDA public guidance is broadly consistent with the 400mg/day
        figure for healthy adults.
      </p>
      <br />
      <a href="https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.2903/j.efsa.2015.4102" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the EFSA scientific opinion on caffeine safety →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Who Should Be Cautious</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Per EFSA guidance, pregnant and breastfeeding people should limit intake to 200mg/day. Anyone with anxiety
        disorders, heart arrhythmias, or high sensitivity to stimulants should stay well under the general
        guidance above, and anyone with sleep difficulties should be cautious of any caffeine within roughly 6+
        hours of bedtime, per the research above. If it's less about caffeine and more about timing your bedtime
        itself, our <a href="/calculators/sleep/">Sleep Cycle Calculator</a> can help you work backward from a
        wake-up time in full 90-minute sleep cycles.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <a href="https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.2903/j.efsa.2015.4102" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EFSA — Scientific Opinion on the Safety of Caffeine</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
